WEBVTT

00:00:00.010 --> 00:00:06.087
This slide emphasizes a software-design principle that matters in real RAG systems.

00:00:06.087 --> 00:00:08.787
The retriever should be replaceable.

00:00:08.787 --> 00:00:12.332
Our first version called a NumPy-based function.

00:00:12.332 --> 00:00:15.432
The Chroma version calls a database query.

00:00:15.432 --> 00:00:21.997
But the downstream steps—building context, creating the prompt, calling the language model,

00:00:21.997 --> 00:00:26.222
and displaying sources—can remain almost unchanged.

00:00:26.222 --> 00:00:33.062
This separation lets a team experiment with storage technologies without rewriting the whole application.

00:00:33.062 --> 00:00:39.957
[tts-break] For example, a prototype may start with Chroma and later move to Qdrant or pgvector.

00:00:39.957 --> 00:00:46.102
If retrieval is hidden behind a clean function or service, that migration is much easier.

00:00:46.102 --> 00:00:53.737
So when you design your own RAG code, avoid mixing database-specific syntax into every part of the

00:00:53.737 --> 00:00:56.287
application. Keep a boundary around retrieval.

00:00:56.287 --> 00:01:01.637
That same principle is common in production software engineering, not only in AI.
